%I A401007 #15 Oct 18 2026 23:26:01
%S A401007 0,0,1,2,3,4,5,7,8,10,13,15,17,20,22
%N A401007 Minimum height of n lattice points with x-coordinates 1..n such that pairwise slopes are distinct.
%C A401007 The sequence is nondecreasing: Deleting the last point from any valid configuration of n+1 points leaves a valid configuration of n points.
%C A401007 a(n) >= n-2 because at most one pair of points may have the same y-coordinate.
%C A401007 a(n) = Omega(n^(5/4)): Erdős, Graham, Ruzsa, and Taylor (1992) proved that an N X N lattice grid contains at most O(N^(4/5)) points with pairwise distinct slopes. An optimal configuration for a term of this sequence lies in a square of side length N = max(n, a(n)+1), so n = O(N^(4/5)), hence N = Omega(n^(5/4)). For sufficiently large n, this forces N = a(n)+1, giving the stated bound. Thus, a(n)/n is unbounded.
%H A401007 Paul Erdős, Ron Graham, Imre Z. Ruzsa, and Herbert Taylor, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01191203">Bounds for arrays of dots with distinct slopes or lengths</a>, Combinatorica 12(1) (1992), 39-44.
%H A401007 Robert E. Peile and Herbert Taylor, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0898-1221(00)00115-2">Sets of points with pairwise distinct slopes</a>, Comp. Math. Appl. 39(11) (2000), 109-115.
%e A401007 9 points can be achieved with a max height of 8: [8, 4, 1, 7, 5, 6, 0, 3, 8].
%e A401007 15 points can be achieved with a max height of 22: [1, 7, 14, 0, 21, 2, 11, 3, 22, 12, 5, 18, 1, 17, 4].
%Y A401007 Cf. A255709, A236266, A213222, A000769, A393993.
%K A401007 nonn,more,new
%O A401007 1,4
